WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.340
Can one cell produce several cell types?

2
00:00:03.580 --> 00:00:07.480
In a crowded dish, neurons and pigment cells sit side by side.

3
00:00:08.640 --> 00:00:13.400
You cannot tell if one ancestor made both, or separate specialists grew together.

4
00:00:16.500 --> 00:00:19.600
To find out, plate cells sparsely so each sits alone.

5
00:00:20.880 --> 00:00:25.100
Over days, one cell divides into a clone, a colony from one cell.

6
00:00:26.360 --> 00:00:31.360
One division proves little, because short-lived progenitors also divide briefly

7
00:00:31.360 --> 00:00:32.720
before differentiating.

8
00:00:34.100 --> 00:00:37.860
Repeated growth and replating tests whether a stem-cell pool persists.

9
00:00:38.933 --> 00:00:41.553
Changing the dish tests other growth habits.

10
00:00:42.693 --> 00:00:47.213
A normal fibroblast, a connective-tissue cell, divides only when anchored.

11
00:00:48.533 --> 00:00:52.513
In soft agar, a gel with no anchor, normal fibroblasts stop.

12
00:00:53.753 --> 00:00:58.513
A transformed cell, with tumour-like habits, needs no anchor and forms a colony.

13
00:00:59.667 --> 00:01:04.767
Now take one neural crest cell, a migrating embryonic cell, alone in a dish.

14
00:01:06.007 --> 00:01:09.067
It grows a colony of both neurons and pigment cells.

15
00:01:10.387 --> 00:01:13.187
Does that show this cell would have made both in the embryo?

16
00:01:18.850 --> 00:01:24.890
Not necessarily. Growing one cell into a colony tests potential, not fate.

17
00:01:26.570 --> 00:01:29.090
Culture shows what a cell can make under one condition.

18
00:01:30.430 --> 00:01:34.090
In the embryo, route and surroundings might choose only one type.

19
00:01:35.250 --> 00:01:39.450
To learn what it did make, label it in place and find its descendants later.

20
00:01:40.550 --> 00:01:41.930
Two traps can mislead.

21
00:01:43.350 --> 00:01:47.990
Proportions in a mixed culture can shift through faster division or death, without

22
00:01:47.990 --> 00:01:53.930
any fate change. Follow clones instead, counting every cell at start and end.

23
00:01:55.370 --> 00:01:59.290
Dish conditions can also trigger fates hard to find in living animals.

24
00:02:00.730 --> 00:02:05.590
Helper cells boosting colonies show they can help, not that living tissue requires

25
00:02:05.590 --> 00:02:06.590
them.

26
00:02:06.950 --> 00:02:10.250
With those guards, colony assays unlocked findings.

27
00:02:11.650 --> 00:02:17.190
In 1988, Anne Baroffio, Elisabeth Dupin and Le Douarin plated single quail crest

28
00:02:17.190 --> 00:02:21.050
cells. They analysed 243 clones.

29
00:02:22.450 --> 00:02:28.170
Some made one neuron type; others made several neuron types, non-neuronal cells and

30
00:02:28.170 --> 00:02:29.450
pigment-making melanocytes.

31
00:02:30.600 --> 00:02:32.500
Soft agar surprised, too.

32
00:02:33.740 --> 00:02:38.640
A transforming growth factor, or TGF, was thought to come from cancer cells.

33
00:02:40.000 --> 00:02:45.440
In 1981, Anita Roberts and Michael Sporn found such activity in healthy mouse

34
00:02:45.440 --> 00:02:46.440
organs.

35
00:02:47.033 --> 00:02:53.833
In 1983, they showed the original activity was two proteins, TGF-α and TGF-β.

36
00:02:55.173 --> 00:03:02.153
TGF-β raised large colonies only alongside TGF-α or epidermal growth factor.

37
00:03:03.183 --> 00:03:05.643
Colonies also guided reprogramming.

38
00:03:06.783 --> 00:03:13.943
In 2006, Kazutoshi Takahashi and Shinya Yamanaka delivered 24 stem-cell genes into

39
00:03:13.943 --> 00:03:15.083
mouse fibroblasts.

40
00:03:16.263 --> 00:03:21.703
They linked a stem-cell gene to drug resistance, so only cells activating it formed

41
00:03:21.703 --> 00:03:27.623
colonies. Leaving out one factor at a time left four that made induced pluripotent

42
00:03:27.623 --> 00:03:28.623
stem cells.

43
00:03:29.417 --> 00:03:33.137
Every colony reveals what one isolated cell can do.

44
00:03:34.277 --> 00:03:37.657
To learn what that cell did, look back inside the living body.
